## Why Key-Based SSH Authentication Is Required

SSH authentication can work using passwords or cryptographic key pairs. Password authentication requires interactive input, which makes automation impossible. If a script attempts to connect using SSH and the server asks for a password, execution stops.

Key-based authentication solves this by using a mathematically linked pair of keys. The private key remains on the Jump Host and proves identity. The public key is copied to each remote server. During login, the remote SSH service verifies that the connecting client possesses the matching private key.

If the keys match, access is granted automatically without password prompts.

